00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:07.010 align:start position:0%
how does the voting system in the  Council of the European Union work the  council is made up of member state  representatives who meet regularly to  adopt and discuss EU legislation  decisions at council meetings are taken  by vote the council can only vote if a  majority of its members is present 15  out of 28  there are three types of voting  procedure simple majority qualified  majority and unanimity simple majority  voting is used for procedural issues  such as the rules governing council  committees it requires the majority of  member states 15 out of a total of 28 to  vote in favor of the proposal qualified  majority is used for the 80% of EU  legislation adopted through the ordinary  legislative procedure also known as Co  decision a qualified majority is reached  if two conditions are met  55% of Member States vote in favor in  practice this means 16 out of 28 the  proposal is supported by Member States  representing at least 65% of the total  EU population this is the double  majority rule proposals can be blocked  by a group of at least four member  states representing more than 35 percent  of the EU population this is called a  blocking minority not that complicated  but wait there's a catch this qualified  majority rule is in use from November  2014  but until 2017 Member States can still  request to use the previous rule under  this rule each member state has a  certain number of votes based on their  population size reaching a qualified  majority needs 260 votes out of a total  of 352 from at least 15 Member States  now for your Nana mati I guess it's  pretty self-explanatory  it requires everyone to agree or abstain  if a councilmember abstains the other  ministers can still reach unanimity  unanimity voting is used for some  specific areas mentioned in the EU  treaties such as citizenship EU  membership and Taxation after the  council votes on legislative texts the  results are immediately made available  to the public now you know how voting in  the Council of the EU works find out  more on the website at Concilium dr.  Roper dot EU
 

